1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Four keys, searched with different frequencies. The tree is not fixed: only the left-to-right order is.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Three legal trees on the same keys: a chain with 10 on top, one with 40 on top, one with 20 on top.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The weights are 4, 2, 1 and 3 searches out of every ten. Key 10 is asked for most often, key 30 least.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The three cost 23, 21 and 19 on these weights. Same keys, same order, four comparisons apart.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The level of a key is how many keys you compare it against, counting itself. The root sits at level 1.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Cost of a tree = sum over keys of weight times level. On the chain: 4 times 1, 2 times 2, 1 times 3, 3 times 4 = 23.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Divide by the total weight 10: the three trees average 2.3, 2.1 and 1.9 comparisons per search.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The same cost function covers unsuccessful searches too, with one convention change.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Fix the root. Everything smaller goes left, everything larger goes right, and each side is itself a search tree.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
A key one level deeper costs exactly its weight more. So moving a whole subtree down adds its total weight once.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
So cost of tree = cost of left + cost of right + weight of the whole range.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
That is optimal substructure: the best tree for a range uses the best trees for its two parts.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
C of i and j is the cheapest cost for keys i to j. One cell, one range.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
C of i and i is just the weight of key i: one key, one comparison.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
C of i and j = weight of the range + the smallest of C(i,r-1) + C(r+1,j) over every root r.

16
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Every subproblem is solved once, and the answer is one cell: C of 1 and n.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
The table is a triangle: n times n plus one over two cells, which is 10 for four keys.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The first diagonal is free: each cell is the weight of one key.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Length two needs length one. Length three needs length two. Length four needs everything below it.

20
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The work per cell is proportional to its length, so the total is a cubic in n.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Keys 10, 20, 30, 40 with weights 4, 2, 1, 3. The diagonal is 4, 2, 1, 3.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Length two: range 1 to 2 costs 8, range 2 to 3 costs 4, range 3 to 4 costs 5.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Length three: 11 and 10. Length four: the four roots score 20, 19, 21, 21.

24
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Root 20, with 10 on the left and 40 on the right carrying 30. Levels 1, 2, 2, 3. Check: 19.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
The tempting rule: put the most probable key at the root, then repeat. On this instance it gives 20.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The optimal tree scores 19. Greedy is one comparison worse, and that is enough to be wrong.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
A second instance: weights 3, 3, 1, 1. Greedy gives 16, the optimum is 14 with root 20.

28
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The failure is structural: the root choice is paid for by every key below it, so it cannot be judged alone.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Keep a second table: R of i and j is the root that won that cell.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
R of 1 and 4 is 2, so 20 is the root. R of 1 and 1 is 1. R of 3 and 4 is 4.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Recompute the cost from the rebuilt tree. It must equal the table entry, or one of the two is wrong.

32
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Ties are real: with weights 5, 45, 45, 5 there are two optimal roots, so two optimal trees.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Huffman also minimises a weighted depth. But its tree has no ordering constraint at all.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Huffman merges the two lightest weights repeatedly. On 5, 45, 45, 5 it gives a cost of 165.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Huffman is not a search tree: it puts keys 1 and 4 in one subtree and keys 2 and 3 in the other.

36
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:30,000
The order-respecting cousin of Huffman is alphabetic coding, and it is not what GATE asks for here.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Step one: check that the keys are sorted and that every key has a weight. Step two: build the triangle.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Step three: fill by increasing length. Step four: record the winning root in every cell.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Step five: rebuild the tree, then re-add weight times level and check it against the top cell.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The traps: greedy root, edge-depth, a missing range weight, and assuming the answer is unique.

